"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][5]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M5-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M5-L1 – Signals, Systems and Linearity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Signals and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Sine functions, exponential functions\nand complex phasors from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>.\nThe step response from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>\nserves as a first example of a system.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish continuous-time and\ndiscrete-time signals and test a signal model for linearity, time\ninvariance, causality and boundedness.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A measured voltage and its samples<\/h2>\n<p>A vibration sensor supplies a voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat every instant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a sampling interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe computer receives only the sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=x(nT_\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an integer. Consecutive elements of the sequence are separated by an\ninterval of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\mathrm s=1\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[4]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to the time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHereafter, parentheses denote continuous time and square brackets denote\ndiscrete time.<\/p>\n<p>Time discretisation determines when a quantity is acquired.\nQuantisation determines which values the digital representation can\ntake. A real-valued sequence in a model can therefore already be\ndiscrete in time before an analogue-to-digital converter maps it to\nnumbers with finite resolution.<\/p>\n<p>A system\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaps an input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto an output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=T\\{x\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe initial state is also part of this mapping. When characterising a\ncircuit as a system for input signals, we first set its dynamic state to\nzero. A precharged capacitor would produce an additional free\nresponse.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four distinct properties<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Linearity.<\/strong> For input signals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1,x_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand constant factors\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following must hold:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\{a x_1+b x_2\\}=aT\\{x_1\\}+bT\\{x_2\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This incorporates scaling and addition. The equation implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\{0\\}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA constant measurement offset makes the mapping\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\{x\\}=x+c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naffine for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA converter with a quadratic characteristic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\{x\\}=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nviolates scaling because doubling the input quadruples the output.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Time invariance.<\/strong> If the input arrives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsamples later, the output must likewise be shifted by exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsamples. Formally, for the shift operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{n_0}x[n]=x[n-n_0]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe equality\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\{D_{n_0}x\\}=D_{n_0}T\\{x\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds. Time-dependent gain violates this property despite being linear.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\{x\\}[n]=n x[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe results\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n x[n-n_0]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-n_0)x[n-n_0]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare generally different.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Causality.<\/strong> If two inputs are identical up to and\nincluding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe output up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust not depend on how the two inputs continue afterwards. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=x[n+1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis non-causal. Offline processing of recorded data can use such\nlook-ahead values.<\/p>\n<p><strong>BIBO stability.<\/strong> An input that is bounded at all\ntimes must produce a bounded output. This test concerns finite output\nvalues and by itself says nothing about the internal state stability of\nan arbitrary realisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Moving average in a sensor channel<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement computer displays\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=(x[n]+x[n-1])/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe output uses the current and previous values. For the first input\nsamples\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[0]=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[1]=4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[2]=0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[-1]=0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Input sample<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Displayed average<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The expression adds delayed input values with constant weights. It is\ntherefore linear and time invariant. It is causal. If every magnitude\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x[n]|\\le M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe triangle inequality also gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|y[n]|\\le M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe assumption about the value before measurement explains the initial\nvalue here. In an actual recalculation, the first value could be\nweighted twice or processing could begin only once two measured values\nare available. This boundary handling is part of the definition of the\nprocessing operation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_128_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two discrete stem plots: input samples of 2, 4, 0 and 0 volts, output samples of 1, 3, 2 and 0 volts.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The two-sample moving average turns input samples 2,\n4, 0 and 0 V into outputs 1, 3, 2 and 0 V; an earlier reading continues\nto affect the result.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The individual points are samples, not continuous curves. The output\nat index two remains at two volts even though the input has fallen to\nzero, because the previous sample contributes to the average.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which input times are required by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=(x[n-1]+x[n+1])/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nIs the system linear, time invariant and causal?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is an integrator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=\\int_0^t x(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm d\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nBIBO stable for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nUse a bounded constant input.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The average uses the previous and next values. The weights are\nconstant, so the mapping is linear and time invariant. The next value\nmakes it non-causal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(t)=t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlthough the input remains bounded, the output grows without bound. This\ninput disproves BIBO stability.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and further study<\/h2>\n<p>In real sensors, linearity and time invariance often hold only within\nan operating range. Saturation, drift and changing processing are\nseparate effects. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>, we\nuse linearity and time invariance to derive the response to an arbitrary\nsignal from a short test signal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec01/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture 1: Signals and\nSystems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · signal and system properties · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M5-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M5-L2 – Impulse Response and Convolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Signals and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Discrete-time sequences, linearity,\ntime invariance and causality from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a discrete convolution\nby hand, understand its continuous counterpart, and draw conclusions\nabout delay, causality and stability from an impulse response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A system test with a single input value<\/h2>\n<p>The unit impulse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals one at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is zero at all other indices. The <strong>impulse response<\/strong>\nof a linear time-invariant system is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[n]=T\\{\\delta\\}[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEvery finite-length sequence is a sum of weighted, shifted impulses. The\nresponse to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[k]\\delta[n-k]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[k]h[n-k]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sum of the individual responses is called\n<strong>convolution<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}x[k]\\delta[n-k],\n\\qquad\ny[n]=(x*h)[n]=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}x[k]h[n-k].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For infinite sequences, the sum must exist for the input under\nconsideration. The formula does not specify a separate initial state.\nAny output component arising from an initial state is accounted for\nseparately in a particular differential or difference equation where\nnecessary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_014_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An input consisting of two impulses produces two time-shifted copies of the same impulse response with different heights. Superposition yields four output values.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Two time-shifted, differently weighted impulse\nresponses combine through discrete convolution to form the plotted\noutput sequence.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From reading the plot to calculation<\/h2>\n<p>A filter distributes an input sample over three consecutive output\nvalues. Its dimensionless impulse response is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[0]=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[1]=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[2]=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith all other values zero. Let the input be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[0]=4\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[1]=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand zero elsewhere. Write two rows of weighted copies of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand add them column by column:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4h[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin volts<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2h[n-1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin volts<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin volts<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>At index\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe convolution formula gives, for example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot h[2]+2\\,\\mathrm V\\cdot h[1]=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBeyond\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno product is non-zero. For finite sequences of lengths\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat begin and end with non-zero entries, the linear convolution has\nlength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M+N-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe check\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_n y[n]=(\\sum_n x[n])(\\sum_n h[n])<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhere and helps reveal a forgotten boundary value, for example. The\nresulting four values are instantaneous values at four sampling\ntimes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous time, causality and stability<\/h2>\n<p>The continuous-time impulse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a distribution. It is characterised by its sifting property\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int x(\\tau)\\delta(t-\\tau)\\,\\mathrm d\\tau=x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe continuous counterpart is therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x*h)(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,\\mathrm d\\tau.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For an ordinary continuous-time impulse response of an LTI system,\ncausality means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(t)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn discrete time, the condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[n]=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA causal impulse response accesses only present and past input values in\nthe convolution.<\/p>\n<p>For BIBO stability, the discrete convolution test requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_n |h[n]|&lt;\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMultiplying this sum by the input bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields an upper bound for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|y[n]|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the continuous case with an ordinary impulse response, the\ncorresponding condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int |h(t)|\\,\\mathrm dt&lt;\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA system with direct feedthrough can additionally contain a weighted\nDirac impulse. Its contribution is a scaled input signal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Shift the entire input of the table example three samples to the\nright. At which indices do the four output values occur?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Let the causal response be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[n]=2^{-n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the upper bound of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|y[n]|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x[n]|\\le3\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Time invariance shifts the output sequence by three samples. The\nvalues are output for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3,4,5,6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same order.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The series\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 2^{-n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals two. The bound on the output magnitude is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA constant positive input reaches this bound if it is also present for\nall negative indices in the model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and further study<\/h2>\n<p>A single impulse response describes a system completely when the LTI\nassumptions hold and the input is the only excitation. In a recording of\nfinite duration, missing boundary values can affect the processing. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>\nshows how convolution can be replaced by multiplication in the frequency\ndomain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec08/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture 8: Convolution<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nimpulse decomposition, convolution and examples · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M5-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M5-L3 – Fourier Analysis, Spectra and Frequency Response<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Signals and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Complex exponential functions from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a> and\nimpulse responses from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the Fourier series,\nFourier transform and DFT by their respective applications, and\ncalculate the amplitude and phase of a sensor signal after a low-pass\nfilter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time history and frequency representation<\/h2>\n<p>A periodic oscillation with fundamental frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan, under suitable conditions, be decomposed into components at\nfrequencies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is the purpose of the Fourier series. For an aperiodic signal with\na finite integral of its absolute value, one can, for example, use this\nconvention for the continuous Fourier transform:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(f)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,\\mathrm e^{-\\mathrm j2\\pi ft}\\,\\mathrm dt,\n\\qquad\nx(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}X(f)\\,\\mathrm e^{+\\mathrm j2\\pi ft}\\,\\mathrm df<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The inverse formula applies where the necessary conditions are\nsatisfied. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes frequency in hertz,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes time in seconds and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j^2=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the phasor representation of <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L6<\/a>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes angular frequency. In complex representation, a real cosine\nconsists of two exponential functions with conjugate coefficients at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-f_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe initial phase determines their angles. A sinusoidal oscillation of\nunlimited duration does not have a finite integral of its absolute\nvalue. Its continuous spectrum is represented in the generalised sense\nas a pair of spectral lines.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement record and DFT<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsamples at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a discrete Fourier transform with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutput values. Choose<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X[k]=\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x[n]\\,\\mathrm e^{-\\mathrm j2\\pi kn/N},\n\\qquad\nx[n]=\\frac1N\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}X[k]\\,\\mathrm e^{+\\mathrm j2\\pi kn/N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The frequency spacing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f=f_\\mathrm s/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the upper part of the index range, the frequencies can be read as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(k-N)\\Delta f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=2\\,\\mathrm V\\cos(2\\pi n/8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s=800\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe window contains exactly one period. The only non-zero DFT values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X[1]=X[7]=8\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey correspond to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe peak value of the original cosine is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2|X[1]|/8=2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe doubling applies to such a pair of spectral lines other than the DC\ncomponent and the Nyquist frequency. If the data do not align with the\nperiod boundaries, energy of the observed tone spreads across several\nDFT indices with a rectangular window. This is called <strong>spectral\nleakage<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Frequency response and a sensor example<\/h2>\n<p>From the convolution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=x*h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows for the LTI system that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(f)=H(f)X(f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a single sinusoidal component in steady state,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H(f)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscales the amplitude and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg H(f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added to the phase. Consider a normalised RC low-pass filter with\ncutoff frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arctan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(f)=\\frac1{1+\\mathrm j f/f_c},\\qquad\n|H(f)|=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1+(f/f_c)^2}},\\qquad\n\\arg H(f)=-\\arctan(f/f_c).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The calculated peak amplitudes apply once the free response has\ndecayed:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Input amplitude<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Gain<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Output amplitude<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Additional phase<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Desired oscillation at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.8944<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8944<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.7889<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.7889\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>26.57<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-26.57^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interference at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.4472<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4472<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4472<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4472\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>63.43<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-63.43^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>At the input, the amplitude ratio of the desired signal to the\ninterference is two. At the output, it is four. The filter also shifts\nthe phases and reduces the desired signal amplitude. The appropriate\ncutoff frequency therefore depends on the tolerable deviations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_129_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Magnitude and phase frequency responses of an RC low-pass filter with a cutoff frequency of 100 hertz and points at 50, 100 and 200 hertz.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For the RC low-pass filter, gain magnitude falls\nfrom about 0.894 at 50 Hz to 0.447 at 200 Hz; the cutoff frequency is\n100 Hz.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The orange points mark the desired signal frequency, cutoff frequency\nand interference frequency. The gain is a ratio, not an output voltage;\nit must be multiplied by the corresponding input amplitude.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How fine is the DFT frequency grid for a recording with 1000 samples\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the magnitude and phase of the RC low-pass filter at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=f_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8000<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f=8000/1000\\,\\mathrm{Hz}=8\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA finer grid alone does not demonstrate better separability of closely\nspaced components. Observation duration and window shape affect it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7071<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2\\approx0.7071<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the phase is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn steady state, a sinusoid with a peak of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nappears at the output with a peak of approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7071<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.7071\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and further study<\/h2>\n<p>Such a frequency response describes the chosen model within the\nlinear operating range. Changes to the time basis of the data and to\nscaling must be documented for spectra. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>, we\nextend the frequency axis with a complex variable through which free\nresponse components and stability can be investigated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec16/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture 16: Fourier\nTransform<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · transform and frequency representation · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · units 9\nand 17–19 on frequency response and discrete frequency representations ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M5-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M5-L4 – Laplace and Z Transforms as System Tools<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Signals and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Linear differential equations from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>,\nconvolution from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a> and\nfrequency response from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine poles, zeros and\nregions of convergence for simple continuous-time and discrete-time\nsystems, and distinguish causal realisation from input-output\nstability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two transforms for exponential components<\/h2>\n<p>In continuous-time calculations, the complex variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\sigma+\\mathrm j\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreplaces the purely oscillatory axis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm j\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn discrete time,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=r\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j\\Omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin radians per sample serves this purpose. The bilateral transforms\nare<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(s)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}h(t)\\,\\mathrm e^{-st}\\,\\mathrm dt,\n\\qquad\nH(z)=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}h[n]z^{-n}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nserves as the transfer function corresponding to the respective impulse\nresponse. The <strong>region of convergence<\/strong> specifies the\nvalues of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor which the integral or sum converges absolutely. It is part of the\ntransform result. The Fourier response is obtained on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided that this line or circle belongs to the region of convergence.\nPoles are points where the reduced rational expression becomes\nunbounded. Zeros are points where it becomes zero. A fully reduced\ninput-output transfer function may conceal internal state modes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous-time example<\/h2>\n<p>The RC low-pass filter in the model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\,\\mathrm dy/\\mathrm dt+y=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith zero initial value and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=0.01\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhas the causal impulse response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(t)=100\\,\\mathrm e^{-100t}u(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere the factor 100 has units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm s^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the unit step function. Substitution into the integral gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>100<\/mn><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(s)=\\frac{100}{s+100},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Re}(s)&gt;-100\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The only pole is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=-100\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThere is no finite zero. The region of convergence follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(t)\\mathrm e^{-st}=100\\,\\mathrm e^{-(s+100)t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe imaginary axis lies in the region, so the filter is BIBO stable. The\ntransfer function describes the forced response with zero initial value.\nFor a non-zero initial capacitor voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe free response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(0)\\mathrm e^{-100t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis added in the linear model.<\/p>\n<p>The same rational expression, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Re}(s)&lt;-100\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncorresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-100\\,\\mathrm e^{-100t}u(-t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napart from a single boundary point. This expression grows as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\to-\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis impulse response is anticausal and BIBO unstable. The pole value\nalone does not determine it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Discrete-time example<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=0.8y[n-1]+0.2x[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout a free initial response. The causal impulse response is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[n]=0.2(0.8)^n u[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSum the geometric series:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>0.2<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)=\\frac{0.2}{1-0.8z^{-1}}\n     =\\frac{0.2z}{z-0.8},\n\\qquad |z|&gt;0.8.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The pole is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.8<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the zero is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unit circle lies in the region of convergence. The step response for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=u[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=1-(0.8)^{n+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt starts with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.36<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand approaches one. The limiting value agrees with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(1)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For a causal rational input-output system, BIBO stability requires\nthe poles to lie in the open left half-plane or strictly inside the unit\ncircle, respectively. When causality is unknown, examine the region of\nconvergence rather than just the poles.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_130_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two complex planes: the green region of convergence lies to the right of the pole minus 100 in the s-plane and outside radius 0.8 in the z-plane; the imaginary axis and unit circle, respectively, lie within them.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The causal region of convergence lies to the right\nof −100 in the s-plane and outside radius 0.8 in the z-plane; it\ncontains the Fourier axis or unit circle, respectively.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The shading shows the respective causal region of convergence within\nthe displayed coordinate range. The blue unit circle is not the pole\nboundary of the discrete example. For BIBO stability, the decisive point\nin both cases is that the respective Fourier contour lies entirely in\nthe region of convergence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the pole, zero and stability of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)=(1-z^{-1})/(1-0.5z^{-1})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a causal response.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A bilateral Laplace transform has the region\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4&lt;\\operatorname{Re}(s)&lt;-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith the boundaries in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm s^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCan the corresponding system be BIBO stable?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Multiplying by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(z-1)/(z-0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe pole is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the zero is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe causal region\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|&gt;0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains the unit circle. The system is BIBO stable. The zero at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuppresses a steady DC component.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. The imaginary axis has real part zero and lies outside the\nspecified region. The ordinary LTI model therefore lacks an absolutely\nintegrable impulse response.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and further study<\/h2>\n<p>These examples apply to the stated LTI models and regions of\nconvergence. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L5<\/a> we\nconsider the choice of sampling interval before a discrete filter can be\napplied at all.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec06/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture 6: Laplace\nTransform<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Laplace transform, poles and regions of convergence ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec05/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture 5: Z Transform<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nZ transform and discrete systems · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M5-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M5-L5 – Sampling, Aliasing and Digital Filters<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Signals and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Sample sequences from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a>,\nDFT from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a> and\nthe z-variable from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Justify an appropriate sampling\nrate for a band-limited sensor signal, calculate the alias frequency of\ntwo sinusoidal tones and assess a simple digital filter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From sensor signal to numerical sequence<\/h2>\n<p>Uniform sampling produces the samples\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=x(n/f_\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA continuous-time signal with no spectral components outside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-B,B]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis uniquely determined by its ideal samples and can be reconstructed\nideally if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s&gt;2B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis statement assumes an exactly band-limited model, uniform sampling\nwithout timing errors and appropriate reconstruction. The frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called the Nyquist frequency. At the limiting case, for example, a\nsinusoid with a suitable phase shift may be sampled exclusively at its\nzero crossings.<\/p>\n<p>For a complex oscillation, the analogue frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to the discrete angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=2\\pi f/f_\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j2\\pi kn}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k,n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfrequencies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f+k f_\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare indistinguishable after sampling. For real cosine signals, there is\nalso reflection symmetry between positive and negative frequencies at\nzero phase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational experiment: Two tones, the same samples<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s=1000\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na cosine at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand another at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>900<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">900\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduce the same sequence. Their waveforms differ between the samples.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>900<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">900\\,\\mathrm{Hz}=1000\\,\\mathrm{Hz}-100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe angle of the second cosine is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi n-2\\pi n/10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts cosine equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(2\\pi n/10)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_013_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A 100-hertz cosine and a 900-hertz cosine follow different paths between the markers. With uniform sampling at 1000 hertz, both pass through the same markers.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Signals at 100 and 900 Hz yield identical discrete\nsamples when sampled at 1000 Hz, even though their waveforms differ\nbetween sample instants.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> cos, isclose, pi<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>sample_rate <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> n <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> sample_rate<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    slow <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> cos(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> pi <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> t)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    fast <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> cos(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> pi <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">900<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> t)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> isclose(slow, fast, abs_tol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>n<span class=\"sc\">}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">: <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>slow<span class=\"sc\">:+.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">  <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>fast<span class=\"sc\">:+.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The sequence begins with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.809017<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.809017<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.309017<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.309017<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.309017<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.309017<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.809017<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.809017<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the sensor channel contains both tones with the same phase at the\ntime origin, they add after sampling at the apparent frequency of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubsequent filtering to separate the two individual components is\nimpossible from this sequence alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Analogue prefilter and digital postfilter<\/h2>\n<p>The analogue <strong>anti-aliasing filter<\/strong> attenuates\nfrequencies above the intended signal band before sampling. A real\nfilter roll-off requires separation between the end of the signal band\nand the frequencies to be suppressed. A digital filter can attenuate\npossible interference within the represented frequency range using the\nvalues already sampled. For example, the moving average\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=(x[n]+x[n-1])/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1<\/a> has\nthe frequency response<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(\\mathrm e^{\\mathrm j\\Omega})=\\frac{1+\\mathrm e^{-\\mathrm j\\Omega}}{2}\n  =\\mathrm e^{-\\mathrm j\\Omega/2}\\cos(\\Omega/2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It leaves the DC component unchanged and cancels the alternating\nsequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1)^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the shared alias at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.9511<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(\\pi/10)\\approx0.9511<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe filter attenuates the two previously merged contributions equally. A\nsuitable analogue prefilter protects the information about the desired\ntone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What apparent frequency is obtained for a zero-phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>720<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">720<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz cosine sampled at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A measurement contains desirable signal components up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>180<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand interference above that frequency. What minimum requirement does the\nideal sampling theorem impose on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand why is the numerical value alone insufficient in practice?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>720<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>280<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">720-1000=-280\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the real zero-phase cosine, a one-sided representation shows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>280<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">280\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the ideal band-limited model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>360<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\mathrm s&gt;360\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust hold. In the measurement chain, prefiltering, the desired\ninterference attenuation and the actual transition bandwidth must be\nconsidered. A slightly greater value alone does not guarantee these\nproperties.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and further study<\/h2>\n<p>The example isolates aliasing under uniform sampling. Finite\nresolution, timing jitter, and windowing and filtering effects are\nfurther model components. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L6<\/a> we\ninvestigate what averaging achieves in the presence of random\ninterference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/resources/mit6_003f11_lec21/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture 21: Sampling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsampling theorem, spectral replicas and reconstruction · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.003 Signals and Systems, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · units 18\nand 22 on discrete frequency response and quantisation · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M5-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M5-L6 – Random Signals, Noise and Signal Quality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M5 – Bachelor: Signals and Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Expected value and variance from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a> and\nfrequency response from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe random interference as\na measurement model, calculate the effect of averaging uncorrelated and\ncorrelated samples, and specify the signal-to-noise ratio along with its\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement sequence as a realisation of a random\nsignal<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=s[n]+w[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the signal quantity of interest and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrandom interference. In a second recording under the same conditions,\nthe observed history of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges. The model describes a family of possible sequences. A\nparticular recording is one realisation of this family.<\/p>\n<p>A real wide-sense stationary noise process has a constant expected\nvalue over time, finite second moments and an autocovariance that\ndepends only on the lag\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=\\mathbb E[w[n]]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_w[k]=\\mathbb E\\bigl[(w[n]-\\mu)(w[n-k]-\\mu)\\bigr].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For zero-mean white noise,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_w[0]=\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_w[k]=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn this model, different samples are uncorrelated. In general,\nindependence follows only with additional distributional assumptions,\nsuch as a joint Gaussian distribution. A constant measurement offset is\na different effect. Averaging does not change its contribution to the\nexpected value. A common diagnostic procedure is to compare the mean and\ndispersion in successive windows of equal length. Strongly varying\nstatistics count against the simple stationary model. Approximately\nequal window statistics, however, do not guarantee stationarity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Averaging and signal-to-noise ratio<\/h2>\n<p>For a measured value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat is constant during the observation period, assume that the error\nhas zero mean, is uncorrelated from sample to sample and has standard\ndeviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=0.2\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a block of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsamples,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis estimated:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">𝔼<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=\\frac1N\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x[n],\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[\\bar x]=s,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar x)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The variance formula follows because the variance of the sum in this\ncase is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis squared. The output standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing the ratio of squared signal value to mean squared noise value, the\ninput yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>13.98<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SNR}=\\frac{s^2}{\\sigma^2}=25,\\qquad\n\\mathrm{SNR}_{\\mathrm{dB}}=10\\log_{10}(25)\\approx13.98\\,\\mathrm{dB}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At the output, the corresponding values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2/(0.04\\,\\mathrm V)^2=625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>27.96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">27.96\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor voltage quantities, this conversion to a power ratio assumes the\nsame reference conditions for signal and noise. In other applications,\nthe measurement bandwidth, measurement location and definition of power\nmust be documented.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Correlation and filter selection<\/h2>\n<p>In general, block averaging has the noise variance<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>w<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>N<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar w)=\\frac1{N^2}\n\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\\sum_{m=0}^{N-1}C_w[n-m].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a completely shared random offset, every pair has the same\ncovariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe variance of the mean then remains equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPositive correlation can substantially reduce the expected improvement.\nAt the same time, a longer averaging window delays the response to\nactual changes in the signal. For narrowband interference, a\nfrequency-selective filter may be more suitable, provided that the\ndesired signal carries little energy at the interference frequency. An\nLTI filter with frequency response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmultiplies the power spectral density of stationary input noise by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|H|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhen the filter and the spectral representation are defined for the\nprocess.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_131_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Standard deviation curves for uncorrelated samples and for a completely shared random offset; marked value of 0.04 volts for 25 uncorrelated samples.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Averaging increasingly many samples reduces\nuncorrelated noise, while a random offset shared by every sample is not\nreduced.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The falling curve represents the theoretical standard deviation of\nthe mean, not the dispersion of the individual samples. The horizontal\nline describes the extreme case of a completely shared random offset.\nBoth curves assume the same individual-sample variance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many uncorrelated samples with equal variance are needed to\nreduce the noise standard deviation to one tenth of the individual\nvalue?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two error samples have variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand correlation coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is the variance of their mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the model, the standard deviation decreases by a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReaching\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe SNR gain with a constant desired signal is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ρ<\/mi><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}((w[0]+w[1])/2)=(2\\sigma^2+2\\rho\\sigma^2)/4=0.8\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor uncorrelated samples, it would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and further study<\/h2>\n<p>The example assumes a constant desired signal and known error\nproperties. Stationarity and the zero-mean assumption must be checked in\nthe presence of drift, mains hum or a changing environment. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L4<\/a>\nextends filtering and estimation of noisy measurements in an electrical\nmeasurement chain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-011-signals-systems-and-inference-spring-2018/resources/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.011 Signals, Systems and Inference, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· units 15–19 on random processes, stationarity, LTI filtering and power\nspectral density · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
